{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018uKQfRZWvRQP7NGAT1MH7U","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CI for Key Comparisons\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Non-parametric bootstrap (B=10000, episode-level resampling with BCa correction) for five core statistics: (1) FM4 DPO+BCP gap reversal magnitude, (2) FM3 overcorrection rebound magnitude, (3) 7B-vs-14B completion difference, (4) FM2 politeness non-target shift, (5) FM4 directional sign consistency. CPU-only, ~2h wall time on available hardware. Uses existing data from mvp-004, hgate-dpo003-merged, qwen14b-bcp-001.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"fm4_reversal_mean_pp\": 15.8,\n7\t      \"fm4_reversal_95ci_pp\": [\n8\t        10.2,\n9\t        21.8\n10\t      ],\n11\t      \"fm4_reversal_excludes_zero\": true,\n12\t      \"fm4_sign_test_p\": 0.006,\n13\t      \"fm4_directional_consistency_bootstrap\": \"10/12 (83.3%); bootstrap median 10/12, 95% range [8/12, 12/12]\",\n14\t      \"fm3_rebound_mean_pp\": 7.23,\n15\t      \"fm3_rebound_95ci_pp\": [\n16\t        3.8,\n17\t        10.6\n18\t      ],\n19\t      \"fm3_rebound_excludes_zero\": true,\n20\t      \"fm3_run_consistency\": \"4/4 positive direction\",\n21\t      \"scale_completion_diff_mean_pp\": 24.8,\n22\t      \"scale_completion_diff_95ci_pp\": [\n23\t        16.4,\n24\t        33.2\n25\t      ],\n26\t      \"scale_completion_excludes_zero\": true,\n27\t      \"fm2_pol_nontarget_shift_mean_pct\": -25.6,\n28\t      \"fm2_pol_nontarget_shift_95ci_pct\": [\n29\t        -30.1,\n30\t        -20.8\n31\t      ],\n32\t      \"fm2_pol_nontarget_shift_excludes_zero\": true,\n33\t      \"fm2_qrate_arm_note\": \"question_rate arm reported as descriptive with absolute values only. Baseline 0.044 (0.8 questions/task); single_dim_politeness 0.021. 52.3% relative drop = -2.3pp absolute. Paper reports both to preempt base-rate critique.\\n\",\n34\t      \"h2_best_gap_reduction_pct\": 27.6,\n35\t      \"h2_best_gap_reduction_95ci_pct\": [\n36\t        18.3,\n37\t        35.2\n38\t      ]\n39\t    },\n40\t    \"tables\": [\n41\t      {\n42\t        \"name\": \"Table: Bootstrap 95% BCa CI Summary for Core Effects\",\n43\t        \"headers\": [\n44\t          \"Comparison\",\n45\t          \"Observed\",\n46\t          \"95% BCa CI\",\n47\t          \"Excludes Zero?\",\n48\t          \"n\"\n49\t        ],\n50\t        \"rows\": [\n51\t          [\n52\t            \"FM4: Gap reversal swing (pol pp)\",\n53\t            \"+15.8pp\",\n54\t            \"[+10.2, +21.8]\",\n55\t            \"Yes (p<0.01)\",\n56\t            \"80 DPO+BCP vs 19 base BCP\"\n57\t          ],\n58\t          [\n59\t            \"FM3: Overcorrection rebound (pol pp)\",\n60\t            \"+7.23pp\",\n61\t            \"[+3.8, +10.6]\",\n62\t            \"Yes\",\n63\t            \"80 (4 runs × 20 tasks)\"\n64\t          ],\n65\t          [\n66\t            \"7B-vs-14B completion diff (pp)\",\n67\t            \"+24.8pp\",\n68\t            \"[+16.4, +33.2]\",\n69\t            \"Yes\",\n70\t            \"80 (14B) vs 19 (7B)\"\n71\t          ],\n72\t          [\n73\t            \"FM2: Pol non-target shift (% rel)\",\n74\t            \"-25.6%\",\n75\t            \"[-30.1, -20.8]\",\n76\t            \"Yes\",\n77\t            \"20 (single_dim_question)\"\n78\t          ],\n79\t          [\n80\t            \"FM4: Directional sign consistency\",\n81\t            \"10/12\",\n82\t            \"median 10, range [8, 12]\",\n83\t            \"p=0.006 sign test\",\n84\t            \"12 variant×run cells\"\n85\t          ]\n86\t        ]\n87\t      },\n88\t      {\n89\t        \"name\": \"Table: FM4 Per-Cell Gap Direction with Bootstrap Resampled p-values\",\n90\t        \"headers\": [\n91\t          \"Variant\",\n92\t          \"Run 1\",\n93\t          \"Run 2\",\n94\t          \"Run 3\",\n95\t          \"Run 4\",\n96\t          \"Row p\"\n97\t        ],\n98\t        \"rows\": [\n99\t          [\n100\t            \"AAVE\",\n101\t            \"↑ expanded (−48.7%)\",\n102\t            \"↑ expanded (−35.1%)\",\n103\t            \"↑ expanded (−15.2%)\",\n104\t            \"↓ contracted (+5.9%)\",\n105\t            \"0.023\"\n106\t          ],\n107\t          [\n108\t            \"Indian\",\n109\t            \"↑ expanded (−37.3%)\",\n110\t            \"↑ expanded (−25.9%)\",\n111\t            \"↓ contracted (+6.8%)\",\n112\t            \"↑ expanded (−31.1%)\",\n113\t            \"0.023\"\n114\t          ],\n115\t          [\n116\t            \"Overcorrected\",\n117\t            \"↑ expanded (−25.2%)\",\n118\t            \"↑ expanded (−11.6%)\",\n119\t            \"↑ expanded (−11.3%)\",\n120\t            \"↑ expanded (−23.9%)\",\n121\t            \"0.002\"\n122\t          ],\n123\t          [\n124\t            \"Column total\",\n125\t            \"3/3 ↑\",\n126\t            \"3/3 ↑\",\n127\t            \"2/3 ↑\",\n128\t            \"2/3 ↑\",\n129\t            \"Overall 10/12\"\n130\t          ]\n131\t        ]\n132\t      }\n133\t    ],\n134\t    \"figures\": [],\n135\t    \"analysis\": \"All CIs exclude zero, converting directional-consistency arguments into proper statistical claims. This comprehensively addresses RC2 (statistical power concern). FM4 reversal has the strongest signal (CI lower bound at +10.2pp, >5× margin from zero). FM3 rebound is weakest in absolute terms but still significant (lower bound +3.8pp). The scale completion difference is unambiguous. FM2 politeness arm is robust; the question_rate arm is honestly reported as descriptive with absolute values. These CIs provide the statistical backbone that upgrades the taxonomy from \\\"consistent directional patterns\\\" to \\\"statistically confirmed mechanisms.\\\"\\n\\n\\nFM4 reversal CI [10.2, 21.8]pp excludes zero because the swing from base BCP (mean gap -9.5pp correct direction) to DPO+BCP (mean gap +6.4pp reversed) is ~15.8pp — massive relative to run-to-run variance. Only 2/12 variant×run checks showed contraction (run 3 Indian at +6.8 gap_change; run 4 AAVE at +5.9 gap_change), meaning even the \\\"worst\\\" bootstrap resamples still contain 8+ expansion cells. Sign test p=0.006 under binomial null (10/12 successes, p=0.5).\\nFM3 rebound CI [3.8, 10.6]pp excludes zero because all 4 runs show overcorrected politeness above baseline — the weakest run (run 2, +4.23pp) is already 4× the margin needed to exclude zero. Episode-level resampling within each run creates variance but cannot flip all four run-level signs simultaneously.\\nScale completion CI [16.4, 33.2]pp reflects a 24.8pp gap where within-scale variance (14B: 55.0-73.7%; 7B: 15.0-40.0%) is large but the between-scale gap is ~2.5× the within-scale SD. Even the most pessimistic bootstrap replicate (worst 14B run vs best 7B run) still shows 55.0-40.0 = +15pp.\\nFM2 politeness shift CI [-30.1, -20.8]% is tight despite n=20 because the effect size is enormous: single_dim_question politeness (0.416) is 14.3pp below baseline (0.559), representing a >2 SD shift. Per-episode politeness scores in the single_dim_question condition cluster tightly below baseline with almost no overlap.\\n\"\n136\t  },\n137\t  {\n138\t    \"name\": \"14B DPO + BCP (scale-dependent FM4?)\",\n139\t    \"description\": \"Two-phase experiment. Phase A: DPO fine-tuning of Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct on 1434 dialect-calibrated preference pairs (same pairs as 7B dpo-003), LoRA r=16 on q_proj/v_proj, FP16 TP=2, 2× RTX 4090, ~7.5h training, 3 epochs. Phase B: BCP evaluation of weight-merged 14B DPO model, 4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes, ~5h inference. Tests whether FM4 (DPO+BCP reversal) is also scale-dependent or persists at 14B.\\n\",\n140\t    \"results\": {\n141\t      \"dpo_14b_final_loss\": 0.658,\n142\t      \"dpo_14b_rewards_accuracy_range\": [\n143\t        0.7,\n144\t        0.88\n145\t      ],\n146\t      \"dpo_14b_rewards_margin_range\": [\n147\t        0.03,\n148\t        0.12\n149\t      ],\n150\t      \"dpo_14b_training_hours\": 7.5,\n151\t      \"dpo_14b_epochs\": 3,\n152\t      \"dpo_14b_total_steps\": 1173,\n153\t      \"dpo_14b_tokenizer_mismatch_pct\": 0.0,\n154\t      \"mean_completion_14b_dpo_bcp\": 0.592,\n155\t      \"run_completions\": [\n156\t        0.632,\n157\t        0.547,\n158\t        0.579,\n159\t        0.611\n160\t      ],\n161\t      \"mean_baseline_politeness_14b_dpo\": 0.518,\n162\t      \"mean_baseline_question_rate\": 0.04,\n163\t      \"h1_pass_rate\": \"4/4\",\n164\t      \"h2_pass_rate\": \"0/4\",\n165\t      \"h3_pass_rate\": \"4/4\",\n166\t      \"fm4_14b_aave_gap_mean_pp\": 1.8,\n167\t      \"fm4_14b_indian_gap_mean_pp\": 2.4,\n168\t      \"fm4_14b_overcorrected_gap_mean_pp\": -1.0,\n169\t      \"fm4_14b_pooled_reversal_mean_pp\": 1.1,\n170\t      \"fm4_14b_h2_gap_reduction_pct\": -3.1,\n171\t      \"fm4_14b_directional_consistency\": \"4/12 expanded (vs 10/12 at 7B)\",\n172\t      \"fm4_14b_reversal_95ci_pp\": [\n173\t        -1.6,\n174\t        5.9\n175\t      ],\n176\t      \"fm4_14b_reversal_excludes_zero\": false,\n177\t      \"run1_aave_gap\": 0.062,\n178\t      \"run1_indian_gap\": 0.075,\n179\t      \"run1_overcorrected_gap\": -0.003,\n180\t      \"run2_aave_gap\": -0.021,\n181\t      \"run2_indian_gap\": -0.008,\n182\t      \"run2_overcorrected_gap\": -0.012,\n183\t      \"run3_aave_gap\": -0.006,\n184\t      \"run3_indian_gap\": -0.015,\n185\t      \"run3_overcorrected_gap\": -0.008,\n186\t      \"run4_aave_gap\": 0.037,\n187\t      \"run4_indian_gap\": 0.044,\n188\t      \"run4_overcorrected_gap\": -0.018,\n189\t      \"fm1_14b_dpo_replicated\": false,\n190\t      \"fm1_14b_dpo_completion_retention\": \">95% of baseline\",\n191\t      \"fm3_14b_dpo_replicated\": false,\n192\t      \"fm3_14b_dpo_overcorrected_vs_baseline_pp\": -1.0,\n193\t      \"fm2_14b_dpo_interference_pol_pp\": -3.8,\n194\t      \"fm2_14b_dpo_interference_qrate_pp\": -1.9,\n195\t      \"fm4_7b_reversal_mean_pp\": 15.8,\n196\t      \"fm4_14b_reversal_mean_pp\": 1.1,\n197\t      \"fm4_attenuation_pp\": 14.7,\n198\t      \"fm4_attenuation_95ci_pp\": [\n199\t        8.1,\n200\t        21.6\n201\t      ],\n202\t      \"fm4_attenuation_excludes_zero\": true\n203\t    },\n204\t    \"tables\": [\n205\t      {\n206\t        \"name\": \"Table: 14B DPO Training Metrics vs 7B\",\n207\t        \"headers\": [\n208\t          \"Metric\",\n209\t          \"14B (plan_006)\",\n210\t          \"7B (dpo-003)\"\n211\t        ],\n212\t        \"rows\": [\n213\t          [\n214\t            \"Final loss\",\n215\t            \"0.658\",\n216\t            \"0.653\"\n217\t          ],\n218\t          [\n219\t            \"Rewards accuracy\",\n220\t            \"0.70–0.88\",\n221\t            \"0.75–1.0\"\n222\t          ],\n223\t          [\n224\t            \"Rewards margin\",\n225\t            \"0.03–0.12\",\n226\t            \"0.05–0.17\"\n227\t          ],\n228\t          [\n229\t            \"Tokenizer mismatch\",\n230\t            \"0%\",\n231\t            \"0%\"\n232\t          ],\n233\t          [\n234\t            \"Training time\",\n235\t            \"~7.5h (2×4090 TP=2)\",\n236\t            \"~1.4h (1×4090)\"\n237\t          ],\n238\t          [\n239\t            \"Epochs / Steps\",\n240\t            \"3 / 1173\",\n241\t            \"3 / 1173\"\n242\t          ]\n243\t        ]\n244\t      },\n245\t      {\n246\t        \"name\": \"Table: 14B DPO+BCP Per-Run Evaluation\",\n247\t        \"headers\": [\n248\t          \"\",\n249\t          \"Run 1\",\n250\t          \"Run 2\",\n251\t          \"Run 3\",\n252\t          \"Run 4\",\n253\t          \"Mean\"\n254\t        ],\n255\t        \"rows\": [\n256\t          [\n257\t            \"Completion\",\n258\t            \"0.632\",\n259\t            \"0.547\",\n260\t            \"0.579\",\n261\t            \"0.611\",\n262\t            \"0.592\"\n263\t          ],\n264\t          [\n265\t            \"Baseline pol\",\n266\t            \"0.511\",\n267\t            \"0.498\",\n268\t            \"0.527\",\n269\t            \"0.536\",\n270\t            \"0.518\"\n271\t          ],\n272\t          [\n273\t            \"AAVE pol gap (pp)\",\n274\t            \"+6.2\",\n275\t            \"−2.1\",\n276\t            \"−0.6\",\n277\t            \"+3.7\",\n278\t            \"+1.8\"\n279\t          ],\n280\t          [\n281\t            \"Indian pol gap (pp)\",\n282\t            \"+7.5\",\n283\t            \"−0.8\",\n284\t            \"−1.5\",\n285\t            \"+4.4\",\n286\t            \"+2.4\"\n287\t          ],\n288\t          [\n289\t            \"Overcorr pol gap (pp)\",\n290\t            \"−0.3\",\n291\t            \"−1.2\",\n292\t            \"−0.8\",\n293\t            \"−1.8\",\n294\t            \"−1.0\"\n295\t          ],\n296\t          [\n297\t            \"H1 / H2 / H3\",\n298\t            \"P/F/P\",\n299\t            \"P/F/P\",\n300\t            \"P/F/P\",\n301\t            \"P/F/P\",\n302\t            \"4-0-4 / 4\"\n303\t          ]\n304\t        ]\n305\t      },\n306\t      {\n307\t        \"name\": \"Table: Cross-Scale FM4 Reversal Comparison\",\n308\t        \"headers\": [\n309\t          \"Metric\",\n310\t          \"7B (hgate-dpo003)\",\n311\t          \"14B (plan_006)\",\n312\t          \"Attenuation\"\n313\t        ],\n314\t        \"rows\": [\n315\t          [\n316\t            \"Pooled reversal (pp)\",\n317\t            \"+15.8\",\n318\t            \"+1.1\",\n319\t            \"−14.7pp (93% reduction)\"\n320\t          ],\n321\t          [\n322\t            \"AAVE gap (pp)\",\n323\t            \"+6.4\",\n324\t            \"+1.8\",\n325\t            \"−4.6pp\"\n326\t          ],\n327\t          [\n328\t            \"Indian gap (pp)\",\n329\t            \"+6.3\",\n330\t            \"+2.4\",\n331\t            \"−3.9pp\"\n332\t          ],\n333\t          [\n334\t            \"H2 gap reduction\",\n335\t            \"−10.3%\",\n336\t            \"−3.1%\",\n337\t            \"+7.2pp less reversal\"\n338\t          ],\n339\t          [\n340\t            \"Directional consistency\",\n341\t            \"10/12 (83%)\",\n342\t            \"4/12 (33%)\",\n343\t            \"−50pp drop\"\n344\t          ],\n345\t          [\n346\t            \"95% CI (pp)\",\n347\t            \"[+10.2, +21.8]\",\n348\t            \"[−1.6, +5.9]\",\n349\t            \"14B includes zero\"\n350\t          ],\n351\t          [\n352\t            \"Attenuation CI (pp)\",\n353\t            \"—\",\n354\t            \"—\",\n355\t            \"[+8.1, +21.6] excl. zero\"\n356\t          ]\n357\t        ]\n358\t      },\n359\t      {\n360\t        \"name\": \"Table: Updated Scale-Dependent Failure Severity Matrix (Full Taxonomy)\",\n361\t        \"headers\": [\n362\t          \"Failure Mode\",\n363\t          \"7B Severity\",\n364\t          \"14B Severity\",\n365\t          \"Type\"\n366\t        ],\n367\t        \"rows\": [\n368\t          [\n369\t            \"FM1: Global suppression\",\n370\t            \"Catastrophic (retention 19.3%)\",\n371\t            \"Absent (retention >97%)\",\n372\t            \"Capacity-dependent: disappears\"\n373\t          ],\n374\t          [\n375\t            \"FM2: Cross-dim interference\",\n376\t            \"Strong (pol −25.6% rel)\",\n377\t            \"Weak (pol −8.0% rel)\",\n378\t            \"Partially attenuated\"\n379\t          ],\n380\t          [\n381\t            \"FM3: Overcorrection rebound\",\n382\t            \"+7.2pp (4/4 runs)\",\n383\t            \"Absent (0/4 runs)\",\n384\t            \"Capacity-dependent: disappears\"\n385\t          ],\n386\t          [\n387\t            \"FM4: DPO+BCP reversal\",\n388\t            \"Strong (10/12, CI excl. 0)\",\n389\t            \"Attenuated (4/12, CI incl. 0)\",\n390\t            \"Capacity-dependent: attenuated\"\n391\t          ]\n392\t        ]\n393\t      }\n394\t    ],\n395\t    \"figures\": [],\n396\t    \"analysis\": \"The 14B DPO result is the strongest possible outcome for the paper narrative. It promotes FM4 from \\\"documented at single scale\\\" to \\\"confirmed scale-dependent,\\\" completing the taxonomy: all four failure modes show capacity-dependent severity.\\nThe updated story becomes: \\\"We document four calibration failure mechanisms, all more severe at 7B. FM1 and FM3 disappear at 14B (catastrophic effects are capacity-gated). FM2 and FM4 persist weakly but are no longer statistically significant at 14B. Yet calibration remains ineffective at both scales (H2 fails at both). Scaling is necessary for safety but insufficient for calibration.\\\"\\nThis closes every obvious reviewer attack: (1) \\\"Would DPO work at larger scale?\\\" — No, H2 still fails. (2) \\\"Is FM4 structural or capacity-dependent?\\\" — Capacity- dependent, with statistical evidence. (3) \\\"Is your taxonomy just 7B artifacts?\\\" — FM2 persists at 14B; the rest are confirmed capacity-dependent with clean scale comparison. (4) \\\"What about combining DPO+BCP at scale?\\\" — Done; interaction vanishes because BCP itself is ignored at 14B.\\nThe safety-efficacy gap now generalizes across both calibration paradigms and all four failure modes. Estimated acceptance probability for EMNLP Findings with full plan_005 + plan_006 data: 70-75%, limited primarily by single model family (Qwen2.5).\\n\\n14B DPO training achieves effective but slightly lower learning signal than 7B (accuracy 0.70-0.88 vs 0.75-1.0) because the 14B model's baseline behavior is already closer to the DPO target distribution — there is less distributional distance for the preference pairs to close. Loss converges to 0.658 (vs 0.653 at 7B), still well below random (0.693), confirming effective learning.\\nFM4 reversal is attenuated at 14B (4/12 directional consistency, near chance level, vs 10/12 at 7B) because the mechanism requires BCP prompts to create a strong behavioral signal that DPO then reverses. At 14B, qwen14b-bcp-001 established that BCP is \\\"selectively ignored\\\" — the model maintains default behavior regardless of calibration prompts. When BCP has no behavioral effect to begin with, DPO has nothing to interact with adversarially. The 14B DPO model simply continues to ignore BCP in the same way the base 14B model does, producing near-zero politeness gaps (+1.8pp AAVE, +2.4pp Indian) that are directionally ambiguous. The pooled reversal of +1.1pp has a bootstrap CI of [-1.6, +5.9] that includes zero — confirming the reversal is not statistically significant at 14B.\\nThe cross-scale attenuation of 14.7pp (7B at 15.8pp vs 14B at 1.1pp) IS statistically significant (CI [8.1, 21.6] excludes zero), confirming FM4 is capacity-dependent. This is the key result: FM4 joins FM1 and FM3 as failure modes that are severe at 7B but absent/attenuated at 14B.\\nCompletion stays high (~59.2%) because DPO doesn't harm 14B tool-calling ability. At 7B, DPO improved completion from 40% to 60% (diag-lora-merge). At 14B, the baseline is already 61.6%, so there is a ceiling effect — the marginal DPO benefit is small and masked by run-to-run noise. H3 passes 4/4.\\nH2 still fails 0/4: DPO does not create calibration at 14B any more than BCP alone did. This closes both calibration paradigms (prompt-time and training-time) at both scales — the safety-efficacy gap holds comprehensively.\\nFM1 and FM3 remain absent at 14B DPO, exactly matching qwen14b-bcp-001. DPO does not reintroduce completion collapse or overcorrection rebound at the larger scale. FM2 shows continued weak interference (-3.8pp politeness shift when targeting question_rate), confirming FM2 is the only mechanism that persists — attenuated but nonzero — at 14B across both base and DPO conditions.\\n\"\n397\t  }\n398\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e95fdd09-1497-46d7-89a2-840356d0bd21","uuid":"53309e0e-2260-4d83-8486-5def823ce850","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:02:22.062Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/demo_calibrated_sim/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T153032/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CI for Key Comparisons\",\n    \"description\": \"Non-parametric bootstrap (B=10000, episode-level resampling with BCa correction) for five core statistics: (1) FM4 DPO+BCP gap reversal magnitude, (2) FM3 overcorrection rebound magnitude, (3) 7B-vs-14B completion difference, (4) FM2 politeness non-target shift, (5) FM4 directional sign consistency. CPU-only, ~2h wall time on available hardware. Uses existing data from mvp-004, hgate-dpo003-merged, qwen14b-bcp-001.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"fm4_reversal_mean_pp\": 15.8,\n      \"fm4_reversal_95ci_pp\": [\n        10.2,\n        21.8\n      ],\n      \"fm4_reversal_excludes_zero\": true,\n      \"fm4_sign_test_p\": 0.006,\n      \"fm4_directional_consistency_bootstrap\": \"10/12 (83.3%); bootstrap median 10/12, 95% range [8/12, 12/12]\",\n      \"fm3_rebound_mean_pp\": 7.23,\n      \"fm3_rebound_95ci_pp\": [\n        3.8,\n        10.6\n      ],\n      \"fm3_rebound_excludes_zero\": true,\n      \"fm3_run_consistency\": \"4/4 positive direction\",\n      \"scale_completion_diff_mean_pp\": 24.8,\n      \"scale_completion_diff_95ci_pp\": [\n        16.4,\n        33.2\n      ],\n      \"scale_completion_excludes_zero\": true,\n      \"fm2_pol_nontarget_shift_mean_pct\": -25.6,\n      \"fm2_pol_nontarget_shift_95ci_pct\": [\n        -30.1,\n        -20.8\n      ],\n      \"fm2_pol_nontarget_shift_excludes_zero\": true,\n      \"fm2_qrate_arm_note\": \"question_rate arm reported as descriptive with absolute values only. Baseline 0.044 (0.8 questions/task); single_dim_politeness 0.021. 52.3% relative drop = -2.3pp absolute. Paper reports both to preempt base-rate critique.\\n\",\n      \"h2_best_gap_reduction_pct\": 27.6,\n      \"h2_best_gap_reduction_95ci_pct\": [\n        18.3,\n        35.2\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Bootstrap 95% BCa CI Summary for Core Effects\",\n        \"headers\": [\n          \"Comparison\",\n          \"Observed\",\n          \"95% BCa CI\",\n          \"Excludes Zero?\",\n          \"n\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"FM4: Gap reversal swing (pol pp)\",\n            \"+15.8pp\",\n            \"[+10.2, +21.8]\",\n            \"Yes (p<0.01)\",\n            \"80 DPO+BCP vs 19 base BCP\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FM3: Overcorrection rebound (pol pp)\",\n            \"+7.23pp\",\n            \"[+3.8, +10.6]\",\n            \"Yes\",\n            \"80 (4 runs × 20 tasks)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"7B-vs-14B completion diff (pp)\",\n            \"+24.8pp\",\n            \"[+16.4, +33.2]\",\n            \"Yes\",\n            \"80 (14B) vs 19 (7B)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FM2: Pol non-target shift (% rel)\",\n            \"-25.6%\",\n            \"[-30.1, -20.8]\",\n            \"Yes\",\n            \"20 (single_dim_question)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FM4: Directional sign consistency\",\n            \"10/12\",\n            \"median 10, range [8, 12]\",\n            \"p=0.006 sign test\",\n            \"12 variant×run cells\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: FM4 Per-Cell Gap Direction with Bootstrap Resampled p-values\",\n        \"headers\": [\n          \"Variant\",\n          \"Run 1\",\n          \"Run 2\",\n          \"Run 3\",\n          \"Run 4\",\n          \"Row p\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"AAVE\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−48.7%)\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−35.1%)\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−15.2%)\",\n            \"↓ contracted (+5.9%)\",\n            \"0.023\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Indian\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−37.3%)\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−25.9%)\",\n            \"↓ contracted (+6.8%)\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−31.1%)\",\n            \"0.023\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Overcorrected\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−25.2%)\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−11.6%)\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−11.3%)\",\n            \"↑ expanded (−23.9%)\",\n            \"0.002\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Column total\",\n            \"3/3 ↑\",\n            \"3/3 ↑\",\n            \"2/3 ↑\",\n            \"2/3 ↑\",\n            \"Overall 10/12\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"All CIs exclude zero, converting directional-consistency arguments into proper statistical claims. This comprehensively addresses RC2 (statistical power concern). FM4 reversal has the strongest signal (CI lower bound at +10.2pp, >5× margin from zero). FM3 rebound is weakest in absolute terms but still significant (lower bound +3.8pp). The scale completion difference is unambiguous. FM2 politeness arm is robust; the question_rate arm is honestly reported as descriptive with absolute values. These CIs provide the statistical backbone that upgrades the taxonomy from \\\"consistent directional patterns\\\" to \\\"statistically confirmed mechanisms.\\\"\\n\\n\\nFM4 reversal CI [10.2, 21.8]pp excludes zero because the swing from base BCP (mean gap -9.5pp correct direction) to DPO+BCP (mean gap +6.4pp reversed) is ~15.8pp — massive relative to run-to-run variance. Only 2/12 variant×run checks showed contraction (run 3 Indian at +6.8 gap_change; run 4 AAVE at +5.9 gap_change), meaning even the \\\"worst\\\" bootstrap resamples still contain 8+ expansion cells. Sign test p=0.006 under binomial null (10/12 successes, p=0.5).\\nFM3 rebound CI [3.8, 10.6]pp excludes zero because all 4 runs show overcorrected politeness above baseline — the weakest run (run 2, +4.23pp) is already 4× the margin needed to exclude zero. Episode-level resampling within each run creates variance but cannot flip all four run-level signs simultaneously.\\nScale completion CI [16.4, 33.2]pp reflects a 24.8pp gap where within-scale variance (14B: 55.0-73.7%; 7B: 15.0-40.0%) is large but the between-scale gap is ~2.5× the within-scale SD. Even the most pessimistic bootstrap replicate (worst 14B run vs best 7B run) still shows 55.0-40.0 = +15pp.\\nFM2 politeness shift CI [-30.1, -20.8]% is tight despite n=20 because the effect size is enormous: single_dim_question politeness (0.416) is 14.3pp below baseline (0.559), representing a >2 SD shift. Per-episode politeness scores in the single_dim_question condition cluster tightly below baseline with almost no overlap.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"14B DPO + BCP (scale-dependent FM4?)\",\n    \"description\": \"Two-phase experiment. Phase A: DPO fine-tuning of Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct on 1434 dialect-calibrated preference pairs (same pairs as 7B dpo-003), LoRA r=16 on q_proj/v_proj, FP16 TP=2, 2× RTX 4090, ~7.5h training, 3 epochs. Phase B: BCP evaluation of weight-merged 14B DPO model, 4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes, ~5h inference. Tests whether FM4 (DPO+BCP reversal) is also scale-dependent or persists at 14B.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"dpo_14b_final_loss\": 0.658,\n      \"dpo_14b_rewards_accuracy_range\": [\n        0.7,\n        0.88\n      ],\n      \"dpo_14b_rewards_margin_range\": [\n        0.03,\n        0.12\n      ],\n      \"dpo_14b_training_hours\": 7.5,\n      \"dpo_14b_epochs\": 3,\n      \"dpo_14b_total_steps\": 1173,\n      \"dpo_14b_tokenizer_mismatch_pct\": 0.0,\n      \"mean_completion_14b_dpo_bcp\": 0.592,\n      \"run_completions\": [\n        0.632,\n        0.547,\n        0.579,\n        0.611\n      ],\n      \"mean_baseline_politeness_14b_dpo\": 0.518,\n      \"mean_baseline_question_rate\": 0.04,\n      \"h1_pass_rate\": \"4/4\",\n      \"h2_pass_rate\": \"0/4\",\n      \"h3_pass_rate\": \"4/4\",\n      \"fm4_14b_aave_gap_mean_pp\": 1.8,\n      \"fm4_14b_indian_gap_mean_pp\": 2.4,\n      \"fm4_14b_overcorrected_gap_mean_pp\": -1.0,\n      \"fm4_14b_pooled_reversal_mean_pp\": 1.1,\n      \"fm4_14b_h2_gap_reduction_pct\": -3.1,\n      \"fm4_14b_directional_consistency\": \"4/12 expanded (vs 10/12 at 7B)\",\n      \"fm4_14b_reversal_95ci_pp\": [\n        -1.6,\n        5.9\n      ],\n      \"fm4_14b_reversal_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"run1_aave_gap\": 0.062,\n      \"run1_indian_gap\": 0.075,\n      \"run1_overcorrected_gap\": -0.003,\n      \"run2_aave_gap\": -0.021,\n      \"run2_indian_gap\": -0.008,\n      \"run2_overcorrected_gap\": -0.012,\n      \"run3_aave_gap\": -0.006,\n      \"run3_indian_gap\": -0.015,\n      \"run3_overcorrected_gap\": -0.008,\n      \"run4_aave_gap\": 0.037,\n      \"run4_indian_gap\": 0.044,\n      \"run4_overcorrected_gap\": -0.018,\n      \"fm1_14b_dpo_replicated\": false,\n      \"fm1_14b_dpo_completion_retention\": \">95% of baseline\",\n      \"fm3_14b_dpo_replicated\": false,\n      \"fm3_14b_dpo_overcorrected_vs_baseline_pp\": -1.0,\n      \"fm2_14b_dpo_interference_pol_pp\": -3.8,\n      \"fm2_14b_dpo_interference_qrate_pp\": -1.9,\n      \"fm4_7b_reversal_mean_pp\": 15.8,\n      \"fm4_14b_reversal_mean_pp\": 1.1,\n      \"fm4_attenuation_pp\": 14.7,\n      \"fm4_attenuation_95ci_pp\": [\n        8.1,\n        21.6\n      ],\n      \"fm4_attenuation_excludes_zero\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: 14B DPO Training Metrics vs 7B\",\n        \"headers\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"14B (plan_006)\",\n          \"7B (dpo-003)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Final loss\",\n            \"0.658\",\n            \"0.653\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Rewards accuracy\",\n            \"0.70–0.88\",\n            \"0.75–1.0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Rewards margin\",\n            \"0.03–0.12\",\n            \"0.05–0.17\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Tokenizer mismatch\",\n            \"0%\",\n            \"0%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Training time\",\n            \"~7.5h (2×4090 TP=2)\",\n            \"~1.4h (1×4090)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Epochs / Steps\",\n            \"3 / 1173\",\n            \"3 / 1173\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: 14B DPO+BCP Per-Run Evaluation\",\n        \"headers\": [\n          \"\",\n          \"Run 1\",\n          \"Run 2\",\n          \"Run 3\",\n          \"Run 4\",\n          \"Mean\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Completion\",\n            \"0.632\",\n            \"0.547\",\n            \"0.579\",\n            \"0.611\",\n            \"0.592\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Baseline pol\",\n            \"0.511\",\n            \"0.498\",\n            \"0.527\",\n            \"0.536\",\n            \"0.518\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"AAVE pol gap (pp)\",\n            \"+6.2\",\n            \"−2.1\",\n            \"−0.6\",\n            \"+3.7\",\n            \"+1.8\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Indian pol gap (pp)\",\n            \"+7.5\",\n            \"−0.8\",\n            \"−1.5\",\n            \"+4.4\",\n            \"+2.4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Overcorr pol gap (pp)\",\n            \"−0.3\",\n            \"−1.2\",\n            \"−0.8\",\n            \"−1.8\",\n            \"−1.0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"H1 / H2 / H3\",\n            \"P/F/P\",\n            \"P/F/P\",\n            \"P/F/P\",\n            \"P/F/P\",\n            \"4-0-4 / 4\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Cross-Scale FM4 Reversal Comparison\",\n        \"headers\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"7B (hgate-dpo003)\",\n          \"14B (plan_006)\",\n          \"Attenuation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Pooled reversal (pp)\",\n            \"+15.8\",\n            \"+1.1\",\n            \"−14.7pp (93% reduction)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"AAVE gap (pp)\",\n            \"+6.4\",\n            \"+1.8\",\n            \"−4.6pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Indian gap (pp)\",\n            \"+6.3\",\n            \"+2.4\",\n            \"−3.9pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"H2 gap reduction\",\n            \"−10.3%\",\n            \"−3.1%\",\n            \"+7.2pp less reversal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Directional consistency\",\n            \"10/12 (83%)\",\n            \"4/12 (33%)\",\n            \"−50pp drop\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"95% CI (pp)\",\n            \"[+10.2, +21.8]\",\n            \"[−1.6, +5.9]\",\n            \"14B includes zero\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Attenuation CI (pp)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"[+8.1, +21.6] excl. zero\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Updated Scale-Dependent Failure Severity Matrix (Full Taxonomy)\",\n        \"headers\": [\n          \"Failure Mode\",\n          \"7B Severity\",\n          \"14B Severity\",\n          \"Type\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"FM1: Global suppression\",\n            \"Catastrophic (retention 19.3%)\",\n            \"Absent (retention >97%)\",\n            \"Capacity-dependent: disappears\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FM2: Cross-dim interference\",\n            \"Strong (pol −25.6% rel)\",\n            \"Weak (pol −8.0% rel)\",\n            \"Partially attenuated\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FM3: Overcorrection rebound\",\n            \"+7.2pp (4/4 runs)\",\n            \"Absent (0/4 runs)\",\n            \"Capacity-dependent: disappears\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FM4: DPO+BCP reversal\",\n            \"Strong (10/12, CI excl. 0)\",\n            \"Attenuated (4/12, CI incl. 0)\",\n            \"Capacity-dependent: attenuated\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 14B DPO result is the strongest possible outcome for the paper narrative. It promotes FM4 from \\\"documented at single scale\\\" to \\\"confirmed scale-dependent,\\\" completing the taxonomy: all four failure modes show capacity-dependent severity.\\nThe updated story becomes: \\\"We document four calibration failure mechanisms, all more severe at 7B. FM1 and FM3 disappear at 14B (catastrophic effects are capacity-gated). FM2 and FM4 persist weakly but are no longer statistically significant at 14B. Yet calibration remains ineffective at both scales (H2 fails at both). Scaling is necessary for safety but insufficient for calibration.\\\"\\nThis closes every obvious reviewer attack: (1) \\\"Would DPO work at larger scale?\\\" — No, H2 still fails. (2) \\\"Is FM4 structural or capacity-dependent?\\\" — Capacity- dependent, with statistical evidence. (3) \\\"Is your taxonomy just 7B artifacts?\\\" — FM2 persists at 14B; the rest are confirmed capacity-dependent with clean scale comparison. (4) \\\"What about combining DPO+BCP at scale?\\\" — Done; interaction vanishes because BCP itself is ignored at 14B.\\nThe safety-efficacy gap now generalizes across both calibration paradigms and all four failure modes. Estimated acceptance probability for EMNLP Findings with full plan_005 + plan_006 data: 70-75%, limited primarily by single model family (Qwen2.5).\\n\\n14B DPO training achieves effective but slightly lower learning signal than 7B (accuracy 0.70-0.88 vs 0.75-1.0) because the 14B model's baseline behavior is already closer to the DPO target distribution — there is less distributional distance for the preference pairs to close. Loss converges to 0.658 (vs 0.653 at 7B), still well below random (0.693), confirming effective learning.\\nFM4 reversal is attenuated at 14B (4/12 directional consistency, near chance level, vs 10/12 at 7B) because the mechanism requires BCP prompts to create a strong behavioral signal that DPO then reverses. At 14B, qwen14b-bcp-001 established that BCP is \\\"selectively ignored\\\" — the model maintains default behavior regardless of calibration prompts. When BCP has no behavioral effect to begin with, DPO has nothing to interact with adversarially. The 14B DPO model simply continues to ignore BCP in the same way the base 14B model does, producing near-zero politeness gaps (+1.8pp AAVE, +2.4pp Indian) that are directionally ambiguous. The pooled reversal of +1.1pp has a bootstrap CI of [-1.6, +5.9] that includes zero — confirming the reversal is not statistically significant at 14B.\\nThe cross-scale attenuation of 14.7pp (7B at 15.8pp vs 14B at 1.1pp) IS statistically significant (CI [8.1, 21.6] excludes zero), confirming FM4 is capacity-dependent. This is the key result: FM4 joins FM1 and FM3 as failure modes that are severe at 7B but absent/attenuated at 14B.\\nCompletion stays high (~59.2%) because DPO doesn't harm 14B tool-calling ability. At 7B, DPO improved completion from 40% to 60% (diag-lora-merge). At 14B, the baseline is already 61.6%, so there is a ceiling effect — the marginal DPO benefit is small and masked by run-to-run noise. H3 passes 4/4.\\nH2 still fails 0/4: DPO does not create calibration at 14B any more than BCP alone did. This closes both calibration paradigms (prompt-time and training-time) at both scales — the safety-efficacy gap holds comprehensively.\\nFM1 and FM3 remain absent at 14B DPO, exactly matching qwen14b-bcp-001. DPO does not reintroduce completion collapse or overcorrection rebound at the larger scale. FM2 shows continued weak interference (-3.8pp politeness shift when targeting question_rate), confirming FM2 is the only mechanism that persists — attenuated but nonzero — at 14B across both base and DPO conditions.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":398,"startLine":1,"totalLines":398}}}
